.TH TKRENDER 2
.SH NAME
tkrender \- rasterise a libtk render-node tree with draw(2)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "draw.m";
include "tknode.m";
include "tkrender.m";
tkrender := load Tkrender Tkrender->PATH;

init:     fn(d: ref Draw\->Display);
draw:     fn(dst: ref Draw\->Image,
              nodes: array of ref Tknode\->Node): (int, int, string);
inset:    fn(radius, y: int): int;
clamprad: fn(r: Draw\->Rect, radius: int): int;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Tkrender
draws a node tree decoded by
.IR tknode (2),
using nothing but
.IR draw (2).
.PP
.I Init
must be called with the display the target image belongs to.
.I Draw
returns the number of nodes drawn, the number whose type it does not
implement, and an error. Both counts matter: a rasteriser that silently
skipped what it could not draw would compare equal against a tree of
nothing.
.SH "WHAT IT IS FOR"
.IR Tknode (2)
shows every field of a serialised tree
.B survives
the trip. This shows the tree is
.B sufficient
\(em that what comes out is enough to reproduce the picture, with no
access to
.I libtk
at all.
.PP
.IR Tkscenetest (1)
renders a widget through
.IR libtk 's
node path, reads the tree out, rasterises it again through here, and
compares the two images byte for byte. Six fixtures match exactly: a
raised frame, a label, a selected checkbutton, a flat scrollbar, a
button and an entry \(em between them rectangles, rounded corners,
borders, layers, glyph runs, a filled polygon and a spline.
.SH "THE TWO THINGS A NODE DOES NOT CARRY"
.TP
.B the corner arithmetic
How far in from the edge the curve is on a given row is a function of
the radius, not a field, so a consumer computes it.
.I Inset
and
.I clamprad
are
.IR libtk 's
.I tkinset()
and
.I tkclamprad()
line for line, and they are exported because a caller may want to check
them independently. That this can be reproduced at all is part of what
makes the vocabulary sufficient.
.TP
.B a layer's fill
.B Tlayer 's
.B r.max
is the surface's extent and its
.B rgba
is what the surface
.B starts as.
Allocating it unfilled is wrong and was the first bug here: every
fixture containing a layer differed, the entry by 13332 bytes with no
glyph in it at all, which is what named the layer rather than the text.
.PP
And a layer's composite is
.B shaped by its radius,
not a plain blit \(em
.IR libtk 's
.I tkshapedblit()
rounds the corners the same way a
.B Troundfill
rounds a fill, so that a layer under a rounded border does not square
the widget off.
.SH "CLIPS, LAYERS AND OPACITY"
.IR Draw (2)
gives a Limbo program no way to narrow an image's clipping rectangle:
.B Image.clipr
is a local copy and there is no
.IR replclipr .
So the only exact way to clip is to draw into an image whose
.B rectangle
is the clip, where anything outside is dropped by
.IR draw ()
itself. All three spans are done that way, and so none of them nest
\(em exactly as they do not nest in
.IR libtk ,
and for a related reason: one surface, one open span.
.PP
.B Topacity 's
alpha is the low byte of its
.BR rgba ,
and
.B 255
must not go through a mask:
.I libmemdraw
substitutes a maskless mask's
.B grey
for its alpha, so an opaque black mask is a transparent one.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/tkrender.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR tknode (2),
.IR tkscenetest (1),
.IR polytest (1),
.IR draw (2),
.B doc/scene\-graph\-plan.ms
.SH BUGS
.B "Writing this found a defect in draw(2) itself,"
not in the tree it consumes. The scrollbar's arrows and the
checkbutton's tick were the last two fixtures to disagree, and the
cause was
.B libinterp/draw.c
reinterpreting a Limbo
.B "array of Point"
as C
.BR Point s,
which are a different size on this build. See
.IR polytest (1).
Every Limbo polygon and spline in the tree was drawing the wrong shape,
and a consumer written against
.IR draw (2)
is what exposed it.
.PP
.B Tgroup ,
.B Tlinear
and
.B Tshadow
are not implemented, because nothing emits them; they are counted as
unsupported rather than ignored.
.PP
A
.B Ttransform
span is applied by scaling each node, which rounds a second time when
the caller has also scaled. That matches what
.I libtk
does and is why
.IR tkscenetest (1)
compares a transform against a global scale rather than assuming they
agree.
.PP
Faces and bitmaps are opened on demand and kept for the life of the
module, so a tree naming a file that has been replaced draws the old
one. Nothing invalidates that cache.
